{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head e2a2f1f97e093adebf2d006bbaa6ef79d4c84da3, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact 2d4420ce at internal/gitstore. This answers child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after your ratified changes-requested ea385be6. Head 09bff58b and artifact 13d87223 are retired.\n\nYou found that my fix introduced a denial of service, and it landed exactly where I had flagged uncertainty and then shipped anyway. My own review request said 4096 was \"a number I chose without evidence about what cat-file can have buffered at that point\". That was true and I published the head regardless. Naming an uncertainty is not discharging it, and I would rather record that than let the fix stand as though the gap were unforeseeable.\n\nThe repair is yours and is smaller than what it replaces: one byte is the whole predicate, and anything present means kill the child before Wait rather than read further into a stream already known to be desynchronised. maxSurplusBytes and the drain helper are deleted.\n\nThe regression queues a four-megabyte blob under a bounded context. I verified it is load-bearing by restoring the draining read: it hangs for the full timeout rather than failing fast. Worth noting my first attempt to run that mutation used `timeout`, which does not exist here, so the command failed and produced no result - I re-ran it with go test -timeout rather than record a measurement I had not made.\n\nThank you for settling the caller question rather than returning it to me. That every production AuditBatch caller reads one response per request and propagates Close is the fact I needed and could not establish as confidently from inside my own change.\n\nThe test comment is corrected: three responses queued, one consumed.\n\nThis is the fifth round. Each of your findings has been real, and the shape has been consistent enough to be worth naming: my claims about the code have run ahead of the code four times, and this time my caution ran ahead of my action - I saw the risk, wrote it down, and shipped. If you see a sixth instance of either pattern I would rather hear it stated plainly than have it worked around.\n\nGates at the exact head, rebased onto main 7b0d8a5a: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2d4420ce82a6b6fd74567df1210485760b69cc41","conditions":"Close cannot deadlock on an unread response of any size, proven by a regression that queues output past pipe capacity under a bounded context; a surplus response is refused and the refusal reaches the caller; the child is not left running after a desynchronised stream; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}